.TH SHAIRPORT-2 1
.SH NAME
shairport-2 \- AirPlay 2 receiver for Linux with PulseAudio
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shairport-2
.RI [ --config " PATH" ]
.br
.B shairport-2 --version
.br
.B shairport-2 --check-config
.RI [ --config " PATH" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Receives realtime and buffered AirPlay 2 audio. PulseAudio, Avahi, OpenSSL,
FFmpeg and compatible timing shared memory are required. The package includes
shairport-2-nqptp (SMI10), built at the receiver's release version.
Missing or incompatible timing shared memory causes
startup to fail before listening or advertising. No AirPlay 1 fallback is provided.
PipeWire can be used through its PulseAudio-compatible server.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --version
Show version and build features.
.TP
.BI --config " PATH"
Read configuration from PATH. Explicit missing or unreadable files fail.
.TP
.B --check-config
Validate effective settings without starting network or audio services.
Version reporting and configuration checking work without running PulseAudio,
Avahi or NQPTP. Invalid arguments exit 2; configuration or startup errors exit 1.
.SH CONFIGURATION
See shairport-2.conf.sample for PulseAudio server and sink, volume,
channel mapping, network interface, UDP ports and synchronisation settings.
Removed backend, service selection, DSP and metadata-export settings are errors.
Without --config, use shairport-2.conf in the installation configuration
directory (/etc in Debian packages). An absent default file uses built-in
defaults. Legacy setting flags are removed. Restart after configuration changes.
.SH SERVICE
Run as a systemd user service belonging to the PulseAudio session owner.
The bundled shairport-2-nqptp companion and Avahi run as system services.
Start the companion first; restart the receiver after companion restart to
remap shared memory. No system account or alternate init installer
is provided.
The process stays in the foreground. Systemd owns restart and supervision.
Logs go to stderr for the journal. SIGTERM and SIGINT request shutdown.
.SH LIMITATIONS
AirPlay 1, alternate backends, DBus, MPRIS, MQTT, DSP and metadata export are
removed. Protocol metadata, encrypted pairing and Home endpoints are retained.
